President Trump is taking the first  steps toward tougher gun regulations one  week after the school shooting in  parkland Florida the president tweeted  last night we must now focus on  strengthening background checks which  could mean anything from legislation the  NRA supports to legislation it strongly  opposes the president also wants to ban  bump stocks and other devices that can  enable rapid fire shooting Margaret  Brennan is at the White House where the  president will sit down with students  parents and teachers today to talk about  keeping schools safe Margaret good  morning good morning well in the wake of  last week's massacre the White House is  showing a new openness to gun control  measures today the president will hold a  listening session not only with members  of the Parkland community but also those  impacted by past school shootings in  Columbine and Sandy Hook we have to do  more to protect our children the  president told the Justice Department to  take swift action we must actually make  a difference efforts to ban bump stocks  last year were stalled by the NRA and  Republican leaders we think the  regulatory fix is the smartest quickest  fix the NRA disapproved of Tuesday's  directive arguing that banning  semi-automatic firearms and accessories  has been shown time and again to not  prevent criminal activity and punishes  the law-abiding for the criminal acts of  others bump stocks were not used in last  week's attack but a shooter in Las Vegas  did use them in October to kill 58  people in the deadliest shooting in US  history president Trump's personal views  continue to evolve in his 2000 book the  America we deserve mr. Trump wrote I  generally oppose gun control but I  support the ban on assault weapons a  position he reversed while running for  office now in response to the outcry  from students and activists the White  House is signaling it may revisit the  ban on assault style rifles like the  ar-15 firearm used in the Florida attack  we haven't closed the door on any front  and may consider changing the age limit  for those purchasing military-style  weapons it's certainly something that's  on the table for us to discuss  is not clear how many of the parkland  student activists were invited here to  the White House yesterday one of the  more outspoken david hog said that he  had turned down an invitation to come  here in favor of a prior TV commitment  john he explained that the president had  also been invited to that TV town-hall  margaret thanks
 

